\section{Detailed Mathematical Derivations}

\subsection{Governing Hydrodynamic Equations}

We model each basin segment using the depth-averaged shallow-water equations. Let $h(x,t)$ be the water depth, $u(x,t)$ the horizontal velocity field, $z(x)$ the bed elevation, and $g$ gravitational acceleration.

Mass conservation:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial h}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot (h u) = q{\text{in}}(x,t) - q{\text{out}}(x,t),
\label{eq:mass}
\end{equation}
where $q{\text{in}}$ and $q{\text{out}}$ represent controlled inflow and outflow via pumps and weirs.

Momentum conservation:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial (h u)}{\partial t}
+ \nabla \cdot \left( h u \otimes u + \frac{1}{2} g h^2 I \right)
= - g h \nabla z - \taub(h,u) + F{\text{ctrl}}(x,t),
\label{eq:momentum}
\end{equation}
where $\taub$ is bed friction and $F{\text{ctrl}}$ encodes control forces induced by actuation (pumps, gates, weirs).

\subsection{Reduced-Order Basin State Model}

For control design, we aggregate each basin tier into lumped states. Let
\begin{equation}
x(t) = 
\begin{bmatrix}
h_1(t) \\
h_2(t) \\
\vdots \\
h_n(t)
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
u(t) =
\begin{bmatrix}
p_1(t) \\
w_1(t) \\
\vdots \\
p_m(t) \\
w_m(t)
\end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
where $hi$ are effective water levels in each compartment, and $pj, w_j$ are pump rates and weir positions.

The dynamics can be written as:
\begin{equation}
\dot{x}(t) = f(x(t)) + B u(t) + d(t),
\label{eq:lumped}
\end{equation}
where $f(x)$ encodes gravity-driven flows and spillway behavior, $B$ is the actuation matrix, and $d(t)$ represents exogenous storm forcing.

\subsection{Control Law and Closed-Loop System}

We define a state-feedback control law:
\begin{equation}
u(t) = K x(t),
\label{eq:control_law}
\end{equation}
where $K$ is designed to enforce safe basin levels and flow regimes.

Substituting \eqref{eq:control_law} into \eqref{eq:lumped}:
\begin{equation}
\dot{x}(t) = f(x(t)) + B K x(t) + d(t).
\label{eq:closed_loop}
\end{equation}

Linearizing around an operating point $x^\star$ with $d(t)$ treated as a bounded disturbance:
\begin{equation}
\dot{\tilde{x}}(t) = A \tilde{x}(t) + \tilde{d}(t),
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{x} = x - x^\star$, and $A = \left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{x^\star} + B K$.

\subsection{Lyapunov Stability Derivation}

We seek a quadratic Lyapunov function:
\begin{equation}
V(\tilde{x}) = \tilde{x}^\top P \tilde{x},
\end{equation}
with $P = P^\top > 0$.

Its time derivative along trajectories:
\begin{equation}
\dot{V} = \tilde{x}^\top (A^\top P + P A) \tilde{x} + 2 \tilde{x}^\top P \tilde{d}(t).
\end{equation}

If we choose $P$ such that:
\begin{equation}
A^\top P + P A = -Q,
\qquad Q = Q^\top > 0,
\end{equation}
then:
\begin{equation}
\dot{V} = - \tilde{x}^\top Q \tilde{x} + 2 \tilde{x}^\top P \tilde{d}(t).
\end{equation}

For bounded disturbances $\|\tilde{d}(t)\| \leq \delta$, we obtain:
\begin{equation}
\dot{V} \leq - \lambda_{\min}(Q) \|\tilde{x}\|^2 + 2 \|P\| \|\tilde{x}\| \delta.
\end{equation}

This yields input-to-state stability (ISS) bounds and guarantees asymptotic convergence when disturbances vanish, or bounded deviation under persistent forcing.

\subsection{Overshoot and Actuation Latency Bounds}

Let $hi(t)$ be the level in a critical compartment with safe threshold $h{\text{safe},i}$. From \eqref{eq:lumped}, for that compartment:
\begin{equation}
\dot{h}i(t) = fi(x(t)) + bi^\top u(t) + di(t).
\end{equation}

Assuming maximum inflow $di(t) \leq d{\max}$ and maximum actuation rate $|bi^\top u(t)| \leq u{\max}$, we can bound:
\begin{equation}
\dot{h}i(t) \leq fi^{\max} + u{\max} + d{\max}.
\end{equation}

If actuation latency is $\tau_{\max}$, the worst-case level increase over that interval is:
\begin{equation}
\Delta hi \leq (fi^{\max} + u{\max} + d{\max}) \tau_{\max}.
\end{equation}

Imposing:
\begin{equation}
hi(0) + \Delta hi \leq h_{\text{safe},i}
\end{equation}
yields an explicit design constraint on $\tau_{\max}$:
\begin{equation}
\tau{\max} \leq \frac{h{\text{safe},i} - hi(0)}{fi^{\max} + u{\max} + d{\max}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Safety Invariants Under Telemetry Failure}

In the event of telemetry loss, the control law is replaced by a static fail-safe configuration:
\begin{equation}
u{\text{fail}} = K{\text{safe}},
\end{equation}
where $K_{\text{safe}}$ encodes conservative spillway opening, low-power continuous pumping, and safe weir positions.

The dynamics become:
\begin{equation}
\dot{x}(t) = f(x(t)) + B K_{\text{safe}} + d(t).
\end{equation}

We design $K_{\text{safe}}$ such that:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial h_i}{\partial t} \leq 0 \quad \text{for all critical compartments } i,
\end{equation}
under worst-case $d(t)$, ensuring monotone non-increase of critical levels and preventing uncontrolled surge.

These conditions can be encoded as linear or nonlinear inequality constraints on $K_{\text{safe}}$ and verified using formal methods (e.g., model checking or barrier certificates).

